-- title:
--   The favored graph relative to a choice of cones, and its lifted walks
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$ (Lean's `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`). A **configuration** $\Gamma$ assigns to each point $x$ a double cone $\Gamma(x)$ — a symmetric cone with unit axis and apex angle in $(0,\pi/2]$ — and $V^\Gamma[x] = x + \Gamma(x)$ denotes that cone based at $x$. A **block** is any subset $Q \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$; in the intended application a block is the set of lattice points of a cube.
--
--   This module introduces the graph structure on blocks that a *choice* of cones produces, together with the two results that lift walks in it to walks between actual points.
--
--   * A **cone choice** is a map $W$ sending every block $Q$ to a single double cone $W(Q)$; this is the formal content of "choose for every block a favored cone".
--   * Two blocks are **adjacent relative to $W$** when they are distinct and the edge between them is witnessed by the chosen cone of one of its endpoints:
--
--   $$Q \sim_W P \iff Q \neq P \ \text{ and }\ \Big(\forall x \in Q,\ \forall y \in P:\ y - x \in W(Q)\Big)\ \text{ or }\ \Big(\forall x \in P,\ \forall y \in Q:\ y - x \in W(P)\Big).$$
--
--     This relation is symmetric and irreflexive, so it is the adjacency of a simple graph $\mathcal{G}_W$ on the collection of blocks. Connectivity in $\mathcal{G}_W$ through a prescribed family $T$ of blocks is recorded as the reflexive–transitive closure of the relation "$Q \in T$, $P \in T$, and $Q \sim_W P$".
--   * The relevant graph on points is the **lattice graph** $G$ of $\Gamma$: its vertices are the points of $\mathbb{Z}^d$, and $q$ and $p$ are joined when both are lattice points and one lies in the other's cone. The **fibre** of a block $Q$ over a cone $V$ is $\{x \in Q : \Gamma(x) = V \text{ as sets}\}$, the set of points of $Q$ whose type is $V$.
--
--   Two transfer results are proved. The *both-majority lift* says: if $Q \sim_W P$, and $q$ is a lattice point in the fibre of $Q$ over $W(Q)$, and $p$ is a lattice point in the fibre of $P$ over $W(P)$, then $q$ and $p$ are adjacent in $G$ — whichever of the two disjuncts witnesses the edge. The *alternating lift* says: fix $a \in \mathbb{N}$, a family $S$ of blocks, and representatives $\rho(B, i) \in \mathbb{R}^d$ for $B$ a block and $i \in \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$, such that for every $B \in S$ and every $i$ the point $\rho(B,i)$ is a lattice point lying in the fibre of $B$ over $W(B)$. Then for every walk $\mathcal{W}$ in $\mathcal{G}_W$ from $B$ to $B'$ all of whose support lies in $S$, and for every pair of indices $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$, there is a walk $w$ in $G$ from $\rho(B,\alpha)$ to $\rho(B',\alpha)$ when $\mathcal{W}$ has even length and to $\rho(B',\beta)$ when it has odd length, with $\mathrm{length}(w) = \mathrm{length}(\mathcal{W})$, and such that every edge of $w$ is of the form $\{\rho(B_1,\alpha),\ \rho(B_2,\beta)\}$ for some distinct $B_1, B_2$ in the support of $\mathcal{W}$.
--
--   The point of working relative to a choice, rather than with an adjacency witnessed by *some* favored cone, is that a single representative per block must serve both edges incident to it along a walk; the both-majority lift is exactly that guarantee. The alternating labelling is what makes an interior edge of the lifted walk expose both of the indices $\alpha$ and $\beta$, which is the input to the later multiplicity count.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The adjacency relation carries a configuration argument that its definition ignores (it appears as `_Γ`), so relative adjacency depends only on $W$; the configuration is retained only so that the graph reads as attached to $\Gamma$. Blocks are arbitrary sets rather than a structured type, and the clause $Q \ne P$ is needed because the two universally quantified conditions hold vacuously when a block is empty, which would otherwise make the relation reflexive there. Types are compared as *sets* (the carriers of the double cones), not as axis–angle pairs.
